          "tool_use_id": "toolu_016NDnx9PYrZZ52XD7YDp7Vn",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods.\\ntimestamp: 2026-02-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - pattern/HybridSearch\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two independent sources; established pattern in production RAG systems.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n**Key characteristics:**\\n- **Sparse lexical search** (e.g. BM25) excels at exact term matching and retrieves relevant documents based on keyword overlap\\n- **Dense vector search** captures semantic similarity by comparing vector embeddings, finding results that are contextually similar even if they lack exact keyword matches\\n- **Result merging** via reciprocal rank fusion combines both ranked sets into a unified ranking\\n\\n**Use cases:**\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone on queries that mix:\\n- Rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs)\\n- Natural language intent\\n\\nThis makes it particularly valuable in production RAG systems where users may search for specific technical information alongside natural language questions.\\n\\n**Production adoption:**\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems due to its robustness across diverse query types.\\n\\n## Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nReciprocal rank fusion is a proven method for merging ranked result sets from multiple retrieval methods without requiring additional learned parameters. It assigns each result a normalized score based on its rank position in each result set, enabling stable combination of heterogeneous ranking signals.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Internal source on reciprocal rank fusion in retrieval systems (2026-01-01)\\n[^2]: Evaluation of hybrid retrieval combining sparse and dense search methods (2026-02-14)\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
